U3: How","**To do \(30 \div 7\), use “how many 7s fit in 30?”** - \(7\) fits in \(30\) **4** times because \(7 \times 4 = 28\) - Subtract: \(30 - 28 = 2\) - So the answer is **\(4\) remainder \(2\)** (written **\(4\text{ R }2\)**) Want to practice one more: **\(31 \div 7\)** or **\(29 \div 7\)**?",Procedural,,,,,Academic,,,,1767,62,0.000806,Skipped full rating by Level-1 gate: gate_llm,1,skip,Academic,Concept Explanation,gate_llm,gate_llm
