For now, here are **two full-length 5-mark–style explanations** from the most common topics in *Nationalism in India*. Tell me which other topic you want next (Non-Cooperation / Civil Disobedience / Role of different groups / Collective belonging).

---

## 1) Explain how the First World War helped in the growth of nationalism in India. (5 marks)

The First World War created conditions that increased hardship and dissatisfaction in India, which helped nationalism spread among ordinary people. To fight the war, the British government needed more money and resources, so it increased taxes and raised the prices of many essential goods. This made daily life harder for peasants, workers, and the middle class. At the same time, there were shortages of goods because trade was disrupted, and people often faced unemployment and rising cost of living.

The war also led to forced recruitment in many areas. Many men were taken into the army, and their families suffered due to loss of income and insecurity. In several regions, crops failed and epidemics spread, making the situation even worse. As a result, people began to connect their suffering with colonial rule and started questioning why Indians had to pay such a heavy price for a war fought for British interests.

These conditions increased anger against the British and created a stronger desire for self-rule. After the war, instead of giving more freedom, the British introduced repressive measures like the Rowlatt Act. This further convinced people that British rule was unjust. In this way, the war years became a turning point that pushed many Indians towards the national movement.

---

## 2) Describe the main features of the Non-Cooperation Movement and why it was withdrawn. (5 marks)

The Non-Cooperation Movement was launched by Mahatma Gandhi in 1920 to oppose British rule through peaceful and non-violent methods. The movement encouraged Indians to refuse cooperation with colonial institutions and to build strength through self-reliance. One major feature was the boycott of government-run schools and colleges, law courts, and councils. Many students left institutions, and national schools and colleges were promoted. Lawyers also gave up their practice to support the movement.

Another feature was the boycott of foreign goods, especially foreign cloth. People were encouraged to wear khadi and use the charkha as a symbol of self-reliance. Picketing of liquor shops and shops selling foreign cloth took place in many places. Many Indians gave up titles and honours given by the British as a sign of protest. The movement gained huge participation and spread to towns, villages, and different social groups, making it one of the first major mass movements.

However, the movement was withdrawn in 1922 because it began to turn violent in some places. The main reason was the Chauri Chaura incident, where a protest led to the killing of policemen when a crowd set a police station on fire. Gandhi believed that India was not ready to maintain strict non-violence and that violence would weaken the moral strength of the struggle. Therefore, he called off the movement to prevent further violence and to keep the national struggle based on non-violent principles.

---

