A textbook exercise asking students to calculate compound interest and amounts for different principal amounts, time periods, interest rates, and compounding frequencies.
U22: P=10800
U23: Solve
U24: Same as previous
U25: Explain","**It’s compound interest, so each year you earn interest on the previous year’s total (principal + past interest).** That’s why we multiply by \(1+r\) every year instead of adding the same interest each time.

Here \(P=₹10{,}800\), \(r=12.5\%=0.125\), and \(n=3\) years.

Year 1:
\[
A_1 = 10800 \times 1.125 = 12150
\]

Year 2:
\[
A_2 = 12150 \times 1.125 = 13668.75
\]

Year 3:
\[
A_3 = 13668.75 \times 1.125 = 15372.1875 \approx \mathbf{₹15{,}372.19}
\]

Then compound interest is just:
\[
\text{CI} = A - P = 15372.1875 - 10800 = 4572.1875 \approx \mathbf{₹4{,}572.19}
\]

Want me to show the same solution using the single formula \(A=P(1+r)^n\) step-by-step too?",Procedural,5,5,5,10,Academic | Image Upload,,,0.9800,17380,249,0.004145,"The bot consistently provided accurate, complete, and relevant academic explanations and solutions throughout the session, including handling image-based questions. There were no detected failures or user dissatisfaction, and the session fully resolved the user's intents.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
