## Elasticity of Demand (English Notes)

### Meaning
- **Elasticity of demand** shows how much **quantity demanded** changes when the **price** changes. 
- If demand changes a lot due to a small price change, demand is **more elastic**. 
- If demand changes very little even when price changes, demand is **inelastic**.

### Formula (Price Elasticity of Demand)
- **Ed = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in price)**

---

## Degrees / Types of Price Elasticity of Demand

1) **Perfectly Elastic Demand (Ed = ∞)**
- A very small rise in price can reduce demand to almost zero.
- Buyers shift easily to other sellers/brands.
- **Graph idea:** a **horizontal** demand curve.

2) **Relatively Elastic Demand (Ed > 1)**
- % change in quantity demanded is **greater** than % change in price.
- Common for goods with many substitutes or luxury goods.
- **Graph idea:** demand curve is **flatter**.

3) **Unitary Elastic Demand (Ed = 1)**
- % change in quantity demanded is **equal** to % change in price.

4) **Relatively Inelastic Demand (Ed < 1)**
- % change in quantity demanded is **less** than % change in price.
- Common for necessities.
- **Graph idea:** demand curve is **steeper**.

5) **Perfectly Inelastic Demand (Ed = 0)**
- Quantity demanded does **not change** even if price changes.
- **Graph idea:** a **vertical** demand curve.

---

## Factors Affecting Elasticity of Demand

- **Availability of substitutes:** more substitutes → more elastic demand 
- **Nature of the good:** necessities → inelastic; luxuries → elastic 
- **Proportion of income spent:** higher share of income → more elastic 
- **Time period:** long run → more elastic; short run → more inelastic 
- **Habit/addiction:** habit-forming goods → inelastic 
- **Durability:** durable goods (purchase can be delayed) → more elastic 
- **Multiple uses:** more uses → generally more elastic

---

## Graph (What to draw)
- Axes: **Price (P)** on Y-axis and **Quantity (Q)** on X-axis 
- **Perfectly elastic:** horizontal line 
- **Perfectly inelastic:** vertical line 
- **Elastic vs inelastic:** flatter line = elastic, steeper line = inelastic

