U5: why are only final goods are included while calculating gdp","## Why does GDP include only **final goods**?

GDP counts only **final goods and services** to avoid **double counting**.

- **Intermediate goods** are used to make other goods.
- Their value is already included in the **price of the final good**.
- If we count both intermediate and final goods, the same value gets added **more than once**.

### Quick example
Wheat → flour → bread 
GDP counts only the **bread** (final product), not wheat and flour separately, because their value is already part of the bread’s price.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Not Math/Science | Speech to Text,,,0.9800,12952,288,0.003235,"The bot provided accurate, clear, and complete answers to all user questions without any failures or misunderstandings. The user rephrased a question but did not indicate dissatisfaction or correction ignored. The session is fully academic in social studies with speech-to-text usage and no errors detected.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
