U2: According to Bernier Indian society consisted of impoverished people, subjugated by a small minority of a very rich and powerful ruling class. There was no social group or class between the poorest of the poor and the richest of the rich. Bernier thus confidently asserted that there was no middle class in India. He saw the Mughal Empire in the following way:
- The Mughal king was the king of ""beggars and barbarians"".
- Mughal cities and towns were ruined and polluted with ""ill air"".
- Its fields ""overspread with bushes"" and full of ""pestilential marshes""

Bernier argued that all this was because of one reason: crown ownership of land But none of the Mughal official documents suggest that the state was the sole owner of land. For instance, Abul Fazl describes the land revenue as ""remunerations of sovereignty"". It was a claimed by the ruler from his subjects for the protection he provided to them. It was not a rent on land owned by the ruler. European travelers perhaps regarded such claims as rent because land revenue demands were often very high. However, this was actually not a rent or a land tax, but a tax on the crop.
