A Grade 9 physics question asks why an object's weight on the Moon is one-sixth of its weight on Earth and whether its mass also changes.","**The weight changes because the Moon’s gravitational pull is weaker than Earth’s.**

- **Weight** is the force with which a planet or moon pulls an object.
- The Moon’s gravity is about **one-sixth of Earth’s gravity**, so an object weighs one-sixth as much on the Moon.

The **mass does not change** because mass is the amount of matter in the object, and the object itself has not changed.

\[
\text{Weight} = \text{mass} \times \text{gravity}
\]

So, **the weight becomes one-sixth, but the mass remains the same** on the Moon and Earth.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload,,,0.9500,14511,258,0.003586,"The bot correctly answered all user questions with accurate and relevant explanations, handling multiple image uploads well. There were no technical or content failures, and the session fully resolved the user's academic intent. The user sentiment is neutral as there were no explicit positive or negative feedback markers.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
