## 6 tough questions + quick practice answers (both sides)

### 1) **Should schools stop memorization because phones exist?**
- **Favour:** Yes—focus on critical thinking; memorizing is less useful than using information well.
- **Against:** No—memory builds understanding; without basics in your head, thinking becomes shallow.

### 2) **Does digital memory make people less independent?**
- **Favour:** It can increase independence by helping people manage tasks and reduce mistakes.
- **Against:** It creates dependence on devices; if the device fails, the person struggles.

### 3) **Who owns your “memory” when it’s stored online?**
- **Favour:** Practically you own it, and it’s safer backed up than only in your head.
- **Against:** Companies control storage and rules; your life record can be used for profit or control.

### 4) **Is digital memory more truthful than human memory?**
- **Favour:** Often yes—photos, timestamps, and messages reduce confusion and bias.
- **Against:** It can be edited, taken out of context, or faked; “recorded” isn’t always “true.”

### 5) **Does relying on reminders weaken your brain?**
- **Favour:** It frees the brain for higher thinking; tools have always extended human ability.
- **Against:** If you stop practicing memory and attention, those skills can get weaker over time.

### 6) **Is constant documentation harming relationships?**
- **Favour:** It helps accountability and clears misunderstandings (“what really happened”).
- **Against:** It reduces trust and makes people anxious; everyone feels watched and judged.

---

### How to reach your “20 each” requirement (without me writing the whole set)
Use these **4 question stems** to create more (you can make 20 quickly):
- **Trade-off:** “Would you choose ___ over ___? Why?”
- **Limit:** “At what point does ___ become harmful?”
- **Fairness:** “Who benefits most from ___, and who is harmed?”
- **What if:** “If ___ became normal for everyone, what changes?”

