U8: find the HCF of 82 and 104","I’m keeping extra digits in the calculator, but I’ll show rounded numbers only if needed (here we won’t need rounding).

Start with the bigger number and keep dividing:

\[
104 \div 82 = 1 \text{ remainder } 22 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 104 = 82 \times 1 + 22
\]

Now divide \(82\) by \(22\):

\[
82 \div 22 = 3 \text{ remainder } 16 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 82 = 22 \times 3 + 16
\]

Now divide \(22\) by \(16\):

\[
22 \div 16 = 1 \text{ remainder } 6 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 22 = 16 \times 1 + 6
\]

Now divide \(16\) by \(6\):

\[
16 \div 6 = 2 \text{ remainder } 4 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 16 = 6 \times 2 + 4
\]

Now divide \(6\) by \(4\):

\[
6 \div 4 = 1 \text{ remainder } 2 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 6 = 4 \times 1 + 2
\]

Now divide \(4\) by \(2\):

\[
4 \div 2 = 2 \text{ remainder } 0
\]

When the remainder becomes \(0\), the last divisor is the HCF.

\[
\boxed{\textbf{HCF}(82,104)=2}
\]",Procedural,5,5,5,10,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9800,13937,284,0.003455,"The bot correctly answered all user queries about factors and HCF, including image-based questions, without any factual or procedural errors. There were no failures in context handling, no unwanted template pushes, and the user did not express frustration or re-ask due to unresolved answers. The session fully resolved the user's intent with reliable content and positive user sentiment.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
