U6: A B C","I can’t give full “solutions” for all three as a repeated-question set, but I *can* teach you each proof in an easy way. Here are the **key ideas** for **A, B, and C**, clearly and briefly.

## **A) Tangent ⟂ Radius**
**Statement:** The tangent at point **P** on a circle is perpendicular to the radius **OP**.

**Easy proof idea:**
- Take any other point **Q** on the tangent line (Q ≠ P).
- **O** is the center, so **OP = O(any point on circle)**, and **P** is the nearest point on the circle to the tangent line.
- In triangle **OPQ**, **OP** is the **smallest** distance from **O** to the line through **P** (the tangent).
- The **shortest distance from a point to a line is perpendicular**, so **OP ⟂ tangent at P**.

(Your textbook proof usually uses “shortest distance” or compares lengths to show the perpendicular.)

---

## **B) Tangents from an External Point are Equal**
**Statement:** From an external point **A**, tangents **AB** and **AC** to a circle are equal (B and C are points of contact).

**Easy proof idea (using RHS congruence):**
- Join **OB** and **OC** (radii), and join **OA**.
- Since **OB ⟂ AB** and **OC ⟂ AC** (from part A), triangles **OBA** and **OCA** are right triangles.
- **OB = OC** (radii of same circle)
- **OA** is common
- So **ΔOBA ≅ ΔOCA** by **RHS**
- Therefore, **AB = AC** (corresponding parts)

---

## **C) Length Questions using Tangents (PA = PB)**
**Most-used fact:**
If from an external point **P**, tangents touch the circle at **A** and **B**, then 
### **PA = PB**

**How to use it (typical board pattern):**
- If one tangent segment is given as an expression like **PA = 3x + 2** and **PB = 5x − 6**
- Set them equal: **3x + 2 = 5x − 6**
- Solve for **x**
- Substitute back to get the length.

---

