\frac{dp}{dt}=Kt
\]
Rearrange:
\[
dp = Kt\,dt
\]
Momentum changes from \(p\) to \(3p\), so \(\Delta p = 3p-p = 2p\). Integrate both sides from \(t=0\) to \(t=T\):
\[
\int_{p}^{3p} dp = \int_{0}^{T} Kt\,dt
\]
Left side:
\[
\int_{p}^{3p} dp = 3p-p = 2p
\]
Right side:
\[
\int_{0}^{T} Kt\,dt = K\left[\frac{t^2}{2}\right]_{0}^{T} = \frac{K T^2}{2}
\]
Set them equal:
\[
2p = \frac{K T^2}{2}
\]
Solve for \(T\):
\[
T^2=\frac{4p}{K}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\mathbf{T = 2\sqrt{\frac{p}{K}}}
\]

If you share the next question (or any values/units your teacher gave for \(p\) and \(K\)), I’ll help you start that one too.",Procedural,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload,,,0.9500,13780,237,0.003416,"The bot correctly answers all user questions with accurate mathematical and physics explanations, using image uploads as context. There are no detected failures or user complaints, though the user does not express explicit gratitude or positive sentiment, resulting in a neutral user sentiment score.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
