## Discursive Passage (Practice)

**Passage:** 
Many students believe that using mobile phones during study time is helpful. Phones can provide quick access to dictionaries, educational videos, and online notes. They also make it easy to attend classes, submit assignments, and communicate with teachers and classmates. For some learners, apps that track time and set reminders improve planning and reduce missed deadlines.

However, others argue that phones are one of the biggest distractions for students. Notifications, social media, and games break concentration. Even a short break to check a message can make it hard to return to the same level of focus. Over time, this can reduce understanding and memory, especially during exam preparation. Some students also depend too much on ready-made answers instead of thinking on their own.

A balanced view is that phones should be used with clear rules. If students keep the phone on silent, turn off notifications, and use it only for study-related tasks, it can support learning without damaging attention. Parents and schools can also guide students on healthy screen-time habits so that technology becomes a tool, not a problem.

## Questions

1. What is the **main idea** of the passage? 
2. Mention **two benefits** of using mobile phones for studies. 
3. Mention **two harms** of using mobile phones during study time. 
4. What does the writer suggest as a **balanced solution**? 
5. Find a word from the passage that means **“interruptions”**. 
