A handwritten derivation shows the center-of-mass momentum relation and derives the center-of-mass velocity as the mass-weighted sum of the individual velocities.","You’re on the right track: the algebraic step is valid. The fraction
\[
\frac{m_1\vec r_1+m_2\vec r_2}{m_1+m_2}
\]
is not being randomly renamed as \(\vec r_{\mathrm{com}}\); it is the **definition** of the position vector of the centre of mass for two particles. So you may substitute
\[
\vec r_{\mathrm{com}}=\frac{m_1\vec r_1+m_2\vec r_2}{m_1+m_2}
\]
into the previous equation.

Because \(m_1\) and \(m_2\) are constant, \(m_1+m_2\) is also constant, so differentiating the centre-of-mass position twice gives the centre-of-mass acceleration:
\[
\frac{d^2\vec r_{\mathrm{com}}}{dt^2}=\vec a_{\mathrm{com}}.
\]
Thus, the next line follows by replacing the long fraction with the symbol \(\vec r_{\mathrm{com}}\). The main thing to fix is notation: \(\vec F_{\mathrm{com}}\) usually means the net external force on the system, so writing \(\vec F_{\mathrm{ext}}\) may be clearer. The relation comes from applying Newton’s second law to both particles and using the fact that the internal forces cancel in pairs. Your substitution itself is correct—just make sure you state the centre-of-mass definition before using it.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,18383,293,0.004415,"The bot consistently provided accurate, detailed explanations addressing the user's questions about center of mass and related derivations, including clarifications on notation and exam relevance. There were no bot failures or misunderstandings, and the user did not explicitly express frustration or rejection of the bot's answers. The session is a clear academic tutoring interaction with image and speech input.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | same_intent_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
