U12: Noooo","**\(\frac{3}{3}\) is the same as \(1\) (the whole). \(\frac{1}{5}\) is one-fifth of the whole.**

If you mean **Question 3** in the photo: *“A can do \(\frac{1}{5}\) of the work in 4 days and B can do \(\frac{3}{7}\) of it in 8 days.”* (it’s \(\frac{3}{7}\), not \(\frac{3}{3}\))

Then:

A’s 1-day work:
\[
\frac{\frac{1}{5}}{4}=\frac{1}{20}
\]

B’s 1-day work:
\[
\frac{\frac{3}{7}}{8}=\frac{3}{56}
\]

Together per day:
\[
\frac{1}{20}+\frac{3}{56}=\frac{14}{280}+\frac{15}{280}=\frac{29}{280}
\]

Time for whole work:
\[
1\div \frac{29}{280}=\frac{280}{29}\text{ days}
\]

**So they finish in \(\mathbf{\frac{280}{29}}\) days \(\approx \mathbf{9.66}\) days.**

