Assume \(\sqrt{2}\) is rational. Then
\[
\sqrt{2}=\frac{p}{q}
\]
where \(p,q\in\mathbb{Z}\), \(q\neq 0\), and \(\gcd(p,q)=1\).

Squaring:
\[
2=\frac{p^2}{q^2}\Rightarrow 2q^2=p^2
\]

**(Add your first two lines here)**
\[
2\mid p^2 \quad(\text{since } p^2=2q^2)
\]
\[
2\mid p \quad(\text{because if }2\mid p^2,\text{ then }2\mid p)
\]
So \(p=2k\) for some integer \(k\).

Substitute \(p=2k\) into \(2q^2=p^2\):
\[
2q^2=(2k)^2=4k^2 \Rightarrow q^2=2k^2
\]

**(Add your next two lines here)**
\[
2\mid q^2 \quad(\text{since } q^2=2k^2)
\]
\[
2\mid q \quad(\text{because if }2\mid q^2,\text{ then }2\mid q)
\]

Now both \(p\) and \(q\) are divisible by \(2\), so they have a common factor \(2\). This contradicts \(\gcd(p,q)=1\). Hence **\(\sqrt{2}\) is irrational**.

(Those are exactly the right places to insert the four divisibility statements.)",Procedural,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,15704,275,0.003847,"The bot provides accurate, detailed, and progressively refined proofs of the irrationality of root 2 using variables p and q, fully addressing the user's requests. There are no technical or content failures, and the user does not express frustration or correction, indicating successful resolution. The session includes speech-to-text input but no other modality or failure tags.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | near_duplicate_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
