U6: But someone said that u can explain thing with illustrations and diagrams","**I can explain with illustrations, but I can’t create a hand-drawn/ASCII “picture diagram” here.** What I *can* do is give you a clean **diagram description** that you can copy into your notebook and label exactly, and I can also **check your diagram** if you upload a photo of what you drew.

**Reflex arc diagram (how to draw it neatly):**

1) **Draw the spinal cord cross-section (simple oval):** 
- Make an oval/round outline for the **spinal cord**. 
- Inside it, draw a small **H/butterfly shape** in the center = **grey matter**. 
- The outer region around it = **white matter**.

2) **Draw the incoming (sensory) pathway (upper/back side):** 
- On the **back side** of the spinal cord, draw a nerve coming in and label it **sensory neuron (afferent)**. 
- Add a small swelling on this incoming nerve just before it enters the spinal cord: label it **dorsal root ganglion**. 
- The part entering the cord: label **dorsal root**. 
- Put an arrow **towards** the spinal cord to show impulse direction.

3) **Show the connection inside the spinal cord:** 
- Inside the **grey matter**, draw a short neuron connecting sensory to motor and label it **relay neuron (interneuron)**. 
- Mark **synapse** points: 
- between sensory neuron → relay neuron 
- between relay neuron → motor neuron

4) **Draw the outgoing (motor) pathway (lower/front side):** 
- From the **front side** of the spinal cord, draw a nerve going out: label **motor neuron (efferent)**. 
- The part leaving: label **ventral root**. 
- Put an arrow **away from** the spinal cord.

5) **Add receptor and effector:** 
- At the sensory end, draw a small patch of **skin** and label **receptor (pain/heat receptor)** near a **stimulus** (hot object/pin). 
- At the motor end, draw an **arm muscle** and label **effector (muscle)**. 
- Finally label **response: withdrawal of hand**.

**Schematic line (write under the diagram):** 
\[
\text{Stimulus} \rightarrow \text{Receptor} \rightarrow \text{Sensory neuron} \rightarrow \text{Relay neuron (spinal cord)} \rightarrow \text{Motor neuron} \rightarrow \text{Effector} \rightarrow \text{Response}
\]

If you want, upload your reflex-arc drawing (photo), and I’ll tell you exactly which labels/arrows to fix so it becomes “well labelled” for exams.",NA,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload,,,0.9500,13730,265,0.003377,"The bot provided accurate, detailed, and relevant explanations about reflex actions and reflex arcs, including schematic descriptions and labeling instructions. The session included an image upload and the bot noted the handwriting was unclear but still delivered a comprehensive academic response. There were no failures or user complaints, so the session is rated highly for resolution and reliability with neutral user sentiment.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
