A handwritten physics problem asks for the uniform retardation of a car traveling at approximately 126 km/h and stopping within 2 km. The image shows the student's attempted unit conversion and kinematics calculations.
U2: I car moving along a sharght haphway 126 \( \mathrm{km} / \mathrm{h} \). he nrocipit to a stap within a dist. ance of \( 2 w m \). What is the redardation of the car (orrumed unifum).
U3: I have a doubt doubt it is already given the initial velocity that is 35 m/s and tense is 200 m can we find the time by using them I got the time as 5.73 seconds is it correct method","**No—because \(200\ \text{m}\) is the *stopping distance*, not the distance moved at constant speed \(35\ \text{m/s}\).** While the car is stopping, its speed is decreasing, so using
\[
t=\frac{s}{u}=\frac{200}{35}
\]
assumes the speed stays \(35\ \text{m/s}\) the whole time, which isn’t true.

If the deceleration is uniform and the car stops (\(v=0\)), the average speed during braking is
\[
v_{\text{avg}}=\frac{u+v}{2}=\frac{35+0}{2}=17.5\ \text{m/s}
\]
So the time should be
\[
t=\frac{s}{v_{\text{avg}}}=\frac{200}{17.5}\approx 11.43\ \text{s}
\]
(That also matches using \(v=u+at\) with the acceleration from \(v^2=u^2+2as\).)

So **\(5.73\ \text{s}\)** is **not** correct for uniform stopping; it would only make sense if the car kept going at \(35\ \text{m/s}\) without slowing down.",Procedural,1,1,1,2,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,Incorrect Answer | Blurry / Unclear Image | Correction Ignored,0.9500,13324,414,0.003438,"The session is academic with image upload and speech-to-text modalities, and the bot correctly identified the image as unclear. However, the bot gave an incorrect answer by misreading the stopping distance and failed to correct itself after the user pointed out the error, resulting in multiple critical issues and low scores.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
