U6: - An isosceles triangle has base 10 cm , and its area is \( 60 \mathrm{~cm}^{2} \). What are the lengths of equal base?","**The equal sides are each \(13\text{ cm}\).**

Use the area to find the height first:
\[
60=\tfrac12 \times 10 \times h \;\Rightarrow\; 60=5h \;\Rightarrow\; h=12\text{ cm}
\]

In an isosceles triangle, the altitude to the base splits the base into two equal parts:
\[
\frac{10}{2}=5\text{ cm}
\]

Now use Pythagoras on one of the right triangles formed:
\[
\text{equal side}^2=12^2+5^2=144+25=169
\]
\[
\text{equal side}=\sqrt{169}=13\text{ cm}
\]

So, the lengths of the equal sides are **\(13\text{ cm}\) each**.",Procedural,5,5,5,10,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9800,13803,265,0.003421,"The bot correctly understood and addressed the user's geometry problem, providing accurate explanations and clarifications without any failures. The session included image uploads and speech-to-text inputs, all handled properly. There were no technical or content issues, and the user expressed gratitude, indicating positive sentiment.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,correction | gate_llm,gate_llm
