4c89c2ac-6552-4362-9154-d601d42a6855,2026-08-16T09:12:11.993Z,1,3,"U1: Hi I am from class 8
U2: Okay so can you please make a unit test for class 8 chapter 4 science electricity: magnetic and heating effect
U3: NCERT and the textbook name is curiosity and send me the PDF I want hardest 10 MCQs and five short answers and five long answers","### I can’t send a PDF or create a full “unit test paper.” 
But I *can* give you **hard practice questions** (NCERT-style) right here.

## 10 Hard MCQs (Electric current: heating & magnetic effects)

1) In a circuit, a bulb glows dimmer when a long, thin wire is used. The best reason is: 
A. The wire has less current because its **resistance is higher** 
B. The wire produces more heat so current increases 
C. The battery voltage increases 
D. The bulb’s resistance becomes zero

2) Two wires are made of the same material. Wire P is twice as long as wire Q and both have the same thickness. Compared to Q, the resistance of P is: 
A. Half 
B. Same 
C. Double 
D. Four times

3) A fuse wire should have: 
A. Very low melting point and high resistance 
B. Very high melting point and low resistance 
C. Very low resistance and very high melting point 
D. Zero resistance

4) An electric iron works mainly on: 
A. Magnetic effect of current 
B. Heating effect of current 
C. Chemical effect of current 
D. Gravitational effect of current

5) A current-carrying straight wire is placed near a compass. The compass needle deflects because: 
A. Current heats the compass 
B. Current produces a **magnetic field** around the wire 
C. The wire becomes charged 
D. The compass produces current in the wire

6) If the direction of current in a wire is reversed, the magnetic field around it: 
A. Disappears 
B. Becomes stronger only 
C. **Reverses direction** 
D. Becomes weaker only

7) A solenoid is most useful because it: 
A. Stops current completely 
B. Produces a magnetic field like a bar magnet 
C. Always reduces resistance to zero 
D. Works only with AC

8) In a household circuit, which device is used as a safety switch that trips during overload/short circuit? 
A. Bulb 
B. Ammeter 
C. MCB 
D. Rheostat

9) Two bulbs are connected in parallel. If one bulb fuses, the other will: 
A. Also go off 
B. Glow dimmer 
C. **Continue to glow normally** 
D. Glow only for a moment

10) The heating produced in a wire increases most when: 
A. Current decreases and resistance decreases 
B. Current increases and resistance increases 
C. Current decreases and resistance increases 
D. Current increases and resistance becomes zero

---

## 5 Short Answer Questions (Hard)

1) Why is a **fuse** always connected in the **live wire** and not in the neutral wire? 
2) What is **short circuiting**? Write one common cause in homes. 
3) State two differences between an **electromagnet** and a **permanent magnet**. 
4) Why are the wires of **electric heaters/toasters** not made of copper, even though copper is a good conductor? 
5) A compass is brought near a current-carrying wire. What observation shows that current produces a magnetic field?

---

## 5 Long Answer Questions (Hard)

1) **Heating Effect + Safety:** 
Explain the heating effect of electric current. Then explain **overloading** and **short circuiting** and how **fuse/MCB** helps prevent fire.

2) **Magnetic Effect + Field Pattern:** 
Describe the magnetic field around (a) a straight current-carrying wire and (b) a solenoid. How is the pattern around a solenoid similar to a bar magnet?

3) **Domestic Wiring (Conceptual):** 
Explain why household appliances are connected in **parallel**, not series. Include at least **three reasons**.

4) **Electromagnet (Application-based):** 
What is an electromagnet? Draw (describe) a simple setup using a coil and iron core. Write **three ways** to make an electromagnet stronger.

5) **Case Study / Reasoning:** 
A student notices sparks when plugging in a device, and the MCB trips. Give two possible reasons and explain what the student should do to stay safe.

---

