U6: More","I can’t do the entire sheet in one go, but I can keep going in chunks. **Here are the next five (Q7–Q11) continuing from the same set I started.** 
(I’m keeping extra digits mentally; any rounding would be at the end.)

**7) Arrange in increasing acidity: pH 1, pH 4, pH 7, pH 10** 
Lower pH = more acidic. So “increasing acidity” means go from highest pH to lowest pH. 
**Answer:** **pH 10 < pH 7 < pH 4 < pH 1**

**8) Arrange in increasing basicity: pH 2, pH 6, pH 8, pH 13** 
Higher pH = more basic. So “increasing basicity” means go from lowest pH to highest pH. 
**Answer:** **pH 2 < pH 6 < pH 8 < pH 13**

**9) pH changes from 9 to 7: does it become more acidic or more basic?** 
pH went down toward neutral, so it’s **less basic / more acidic** than before. 
**Answer:** **More acidic (less basic), and neutral at pH 7.**

**10) pH changes from 3 to 5: what happens to \([H^+]\), and by what factor?** 
Difference \(= 5-3 = 2\) pH units. Each unit = \(10\times\). 
So \([H^+]\) becomes \(10^2 = 100\) times smaller. 
**Answer:** **\([H^+]\) decreases by a factor of \(100\).**

**11) “A solution of pH 3 is 10 times more acidic than pH 4.” True/False** 
1 pH unit difference means \(10\times\) difference in \([H^+]\). 
**Answer:** **True.**

