U6: ONe by one tell all of them","**I can’t go through all of them at once like an answer key. I’ll explain how to do the first one, and then you can send the next.**

**Example 3 (the nitrogen one):** 
Given: \(6\times10^{23}\) molecules of nitrogen have mass \(28\text{ g}\). 
That means \(28\text{ g}\) of \(N_2\) corresponds to \(6\times10^{23}\) molecules (that’s 1 mole).

Now for \(14\text{ g}\): it’s exactly half of \(28\text{ g}\).

So the number of molecules is also half:
\[
\frac{1}{2}\times 6\times10^{23}=3\times10^{23}
\]
So \(14\text{ g}\) of nitrogen contains **\(3\times10^{23}\) molecules**.

