U3: \begin{aligned}Am\\\nicefrac{-9^{7}2^{6}8^{5^{0}}}{7_{8}}\end{aligned}","It looks like you’re asking to simplify this expression:
\[
\frac{-9^{7}\,2^{6}\,8^{5^{0}}}{7_{8}}.
\]

First, \(5^{0}=1\), so
\[
8^{5^{0}}=8^{1}=8.
\]

Also, \(7_{8}\) means “7 in base 8,” which is still the value \(7\) in base 10. So the expression is
\[
\frac{-9^{7}\,2^{6}\,8}{7}.
\]

Write \(8=2^{3}\):
\[
\frac{-9^{7}\,2^{6}\,2^{3}}{7}=\frac{-9^{7}\,2^{9}}{7}.
\]

Now compute the powers:
\[
9^{7}=(3^{2})^{7}=3^{14}=4{,}782{,}969,\quad 2^{9}=512.
\]
So the numerator is
\[
4{,}782{,}969 \times 512 = 2{,}448{,}880{,}128.
\]

So the value is
\[
\boxed{\mathbf{-\frac{2{,}448{,}880{,}128}{7}}}
\]
(which is already in simplest form since \(2{,}448{,}880{,}128\) is not divisible by \(7\)).",NA,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,12599,235,0.003124,The bot correctly interprets and explains the user's math expression without any factual or procedural errors. There are no failures or issues detected in the conversation. The user sentiment is neutral as there is no explicit positive or negative feedback.,0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
