U5: Integers

Need for negative numbers
We know that the addition of two whole numbers gives a whole number. However, when one whole number is subtracted from another, the result is not always a whole number. For example:

What is \( 3-5 \) ?
What is \( 11-20 \) ?
\( 3-5 \) is that number which when added to 5 gives 3 . As 5 is greater than 3 , there is no whole number which when added to 5 can give 3. Similarly, 11-20 is not a whole number. To answer these and other similar questions, we introduce a new number corresponding to each natural number.

For the natural numbers \( 1,2,3,4, \ldots \).
we have new numbers \( -1,-2,-3,-4, \ldots \)
such that \( 1+(-1)=0,2+(-2)=0,3+(-3)=0,4+(-4)=0, \ldots \).
The numbers \( 0,1,2,3,4 \), etc., and \( -1,-2,-3,-4 \), etc., are all called integers. Of these, \( 1,2,3,4 \), etc., are called positive integers, while \( -1,-2,-3,-4 \), etc., are called their corresponding negative integers. The positive integers are also written as (+1), (+2), (+3), (+4), etc., while the negative integers are also written as \( (-1),(-2),(-3),(-4) \), etc. The positive and negative integers are together called directed (or signed) numbers.

0 is a whole number whose negative integer is also 0 , because \( 0+0=0 \). However, \( O \) is not included in the collection of positive or negative integers. The collection of the integers \( 0,1,2,3,4, \ldots \) is called non-negative integers, which are also known as whole numbers.

Absolute value of an integer
The whole number obtained by omitting the positive or negative sign of an integer (directed number) is called the absolute value (or numerical value) of the integer.

This is denoted by writing the integer between two vertical poles, such as \( |2|,|-3| \), etc. Thus,
\[
\begin{array}{l}
