**2) Given \(\vec a\cdot(\vec b\times\vec c)=6\), \(\vec b\cdot(\vec c\times\vec a)=6\), \(\vec c\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b)=6\), the value is option D: \(18\).** 

Key fact (cyclic invariance of scalar triple product):
\[
\vec a\cdot(\vec b\times\vec c)=\vec b\cdot(\vec c\times\vec a)=\vec c\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b)=6.
\]

Now expand:
\[
(\vec a+\vec b+\vec c)\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b+\vec b\times\vec c+\vec c\times\vec a).
\]
Distribute the dot product; many terms become \(0\) because \(\vec a\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b)=0\), \(\vec b\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b)=0\), etc. The only surviving ones are:
\[
\vec a\cdot(\vec b\times\vec c)+\vec b\cdot(\vec c\times\vec a)+\vec c\cdot(\vec a\times\vec b)=6+6+6= \mathbf{18}.
\]

---

**3) Unit \(\vec a,\vec b\) and \(|\vec a\times\vec b|=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\) ⇒ \(|\vec a+2\vec b|^2\) can be \(3\) or \(7\), so the MCQ isn’t uniquely determined.** 
Since \(|\vec a\times\vec b|=\sin\theta=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\), \(\theta=60^\circ\) or \(120^\circ\), so \(\cos\theta=\pm\frac12\). Then
\[
|\vec a+2\vec b|^2=|\vec a|^2+4|\vec b|^2+4\vec a\cdot\vec b
=1+4+4\cos\theta=5+4\cos\theta.
\]
So it equals \(5+2=7\) or \(5-2=3\). Both are possible.

