U7: Your are not clearing my bout","**Tell me your exact doubt in one line (pick one):** 
1) **Factoring**: “I don’t know how to break \(x^3-1\) / \(x^3+1\) into factors.” 
2) **GCD vs LCM rule**: “I don’t know which factors go into GCD and which into LCM.” 
3) **Verification**: “I don’t see why \(f(x)g(x)=\text{LCM}\cdot\text{GCD}\).”

Meanwhile, here’s the shortest way to remember it:

- If \(f=(x+1)^2(x-1)\) and \(g=(x+1)(x^2+1)\), then 
- **GCD** takes **common factors with the smaller power** \(\rightarrow (x+1)^1\) 
- **LCM** takes **all factors with the bigger power** \(\rightarrow (x+1)^2(x-1)(x^2+1)\)

That’s why \(\gcd \times \text{lcm}\) matches \(f \times g\): the powers add up correctly.

