U5: 18and 19","**18. Prove \( \sec ^6\theta=\tan ^6\theta+3\tan ^2\theta\,\sec ^2\theta+1\)**

Start with the LHS and use \(\sec^2\theta=1+\tan^2\theta\):
\[
\sec^6\theta=(\sec^2\theta)^3=(1+\tan^2\theta)^3
\]
Now expand \((1+x)^3=1+3x+3x^2+x^3\) with \(x=\tan^2\theta\):
\[
(1+\tan^2\theta)^3=1+3\tan^2\theta+3\tan^4\theta+\tan^6\theta
\]
Rewrite the middle terms by factoring \(3\tan^2\theta\):
\[
1+\tan^6\theta+3\tan^2\theta(1+\tan^2\theta)
\]
and again use \(1+\tan^2\theta=\sec^2\theta\):
\[
1+\tan^6\theta+3\tan^2\theta\,\sec^2\theta
\]
So,
\[
\boxed{\sec ^6\theta=\tan ^6\theta+3\tan ^2\theta\,\sec ^2\theta+1}
\]

---

**19. Prove \(\dfrac{\sec A-\tan A}{\sec A+\tan A}=\dfrac{\cos ^2 A}{(1+\sin A)^2}\)**

Start with the LHS and convert to \(\sin,\cos\) using \(\sec A=\frac1{\cos A}\), \(\tan A=\frac{\sin A}{\cos A}\):
\[
\frac{\sec A-\tan A}{\sec A+\tan A}
=
\frac{\frac1{\cos A}-\frac{\sin A}{\cos A}}{\frac1{\cos A}+\frac{\sin A}{\cos A}}
=
\frac{\frac{1-\sin A}{\cos A}}{\frac{1+\sin A}{\cos A}}
=
\frac{1-\sin A}{1+\sin A}
\]
Now multiply numerator and denominator by \((1-\sin A)\):
\[
\frac{1-\sin A}{1+\sin A}\cdot\frac{1-\sin A}{1-\sin A}
=
\frac{(1-\sin A)^2}{1-\sin^2 A}
\]
Use \(1-\sin^2 A=\cos^2 A\):
\[
\frac{(1-\sin A)^2}{\cos^2 A}
\]
Now note \((1-\sin A)=\dfrac{\cos^2 A}{1+\sin A}\) (because \((1-\sin A)(1+\sin A)=1-\sin^2A=\cos^2A\)). So:
\[
\frac{(1-\sin A)^2}{\cos^2 A}
=
\frac{\left(\frac{\cos^2 A}{1+\sin A}\right)^2}{\cos^2 A}
=
\frac{\cos^4 A}{(1+\sin A)^2\cos^2 A}
=
\frac{\cos^2 A}{(1+\sin A)^2}
\]
So,
\[
\boxed{\frac{\sec A-\tan A}{\sec A+\tan A}=\frac{\cos ^2 A}{(1+\sin A)^2}}
\]

