## 1. Trend across a transition series (e.g., 3d: Sc to Zn)

**Key point for exam:**

- Across a period in a transition series, the **atomic radius generally decreases slightly** and then becomes **almost constant** toward the end.

**Reason (write like this):**

- As we move from Sc to Zn, the **nuclear charge (atomic number) increases**.
- The added electrons go into the **(n–1)d subshell**, which shows **poor shielding**.
- Because of poor shielding, the **effective nuclear charge increases**, so electrons are pulled closer to the nucleus.
- Therefore, the **atomic radius decreases slightly** across the series and becomes nearly constant for the later elements.

**Simple exam-style relation:**

- \( r(\text{Sc}) > r(\text{Ti}) > r(\text{V}) > \dots \approx r(\text{Ni}) \approx r(\text{Cu}) \approx r(\text{Zn}) \)

You can also draw a simple curve: 
- x-axis: element (Sc → Zn) 
- y-axis: atomic radius 
- Show a **slight downward curve that flattens** toward the right.

---

## 2. Trend down the group (3d → 4d)

**Key point:**

- On moving from 3d to 4d, the **atomic radius increases**.

**Reason:**

- A **new principal shell (higher n)** is added.
- So the **distance of outer electrons from the nucleus increases**, and radius becomes larger.

Example you can write: 
- \( r(\text{Y}) > r(\text{Sc}) \) 
(Sc is 3d, Y is 4d.)

---

## 3. 4d vs 5d series (Lanthanide contraction)

**Key exam line:**

- The **atomic radii of 4d and 5d transition elements are very similar.**

**Reason (Lanthanide contraction):**

- Between 4d and 5d series, the **4f orbitals are filled**.
- **4f electrons show very poor shielding** of nuclear charge.
- Due to this, the **effective nuclear charge on 5d elements is high**, which **contracts** their size.
- So, instead of being much larger, the **5d elements have radii almost equal to 4d elements**.

**Famous example (always used in exams):**

- \( r(\text{Zr}) \approx r(\text{Hf}) \) 
(Zr is 4d, Hf is 5d.)

---

## Very short exam-friendly summary

You can memorize and write:

- **Across a transition series:** Atomic radius **decreases slightly** and then becomes almost constant due to **increasing effective nuclear charge** (poor shielding by (n–1)d electrons). 
- **Down a group (3d → 4d):** Atomic radius **increases** due to addition of a **new shell (higher n)**. 
- **4d and 5d series:** Radii are **nearly equal** because of **lanthanide contraction** (poor shielding by 4f electrons).

