I’m keeping extra digits in the calculator, but I’ll show rounded numbers only at the end.

For #1: 
A’s rate \(= \frac{1}{20}\) work/day, B’s rate \(= \frac{1}{30}\) work/day. 
On days 1–3, both work: combined rate
\[
\frac{1}{20}+\frac{1}{30}=\frac{3+2}{60}=\frac{5}{60}=\frac{1}{12}
\]
So in 3 days they finish
\[
3\times \frac{1}{12}=\frac{1}{4}
\]
On day 4, only B works, so work done:
\[
\frac{1}{30}
\]
So in each 4-day cycle, total work done is
\[
\frac{1}{4}+\frac{1}{30}=\frac{15}{60}+\frac{2}{60}=\frac{17}{60}
\]

Now see how many full cycles fit into 1 whole work:
After 3 cycles (12 days):
\[
3\times \frac{17}{60}=\frac{51}{60}
\]
Remaining work:
\[
1-\frac{51}{60}=\frac{9}{60}=\frac{3}{20}
\]

Day 13 (both): done \(=\frac{1}{12}=\frac{5}{60}\). 
Remaining:
\[
\frac{9}{60}-\frac{5}{60}=\frac{4}{60}=\frac{1}{15}
\]

Day 14 (both again): they do \(\frac{1}{12}\), which is more than \(\frac{1}{15}\), so the work finishes during day 14. 
Time needed on day 14:
\[
\frac{\frac{1}{15}}{\frac{1}{12}}=\frac{12}{15}=\frac{4}{5}=0.8
\]
So total time:
\[
13 + 0.8 = \mathbf{13.8\text{ days}}
\]
That is **13 days and \(0.8\times 24=19.2\) hours ≈ 19 hours 12 minutes**.

**Answer for #1: \(\mathbf{13.8}\) days (≈ 13 days 19 hours 12 minutes).**

